 bats to pangolins but that  uh remains to be determined and um the  original source of the virus  can take quite a while to determine in  the case of  the first version of sas it took i think  over a decade to verify  the transmission realm  so since the first whole genome of sara  cov2 was published back in  in mid-january scientists have sequenced  tens of thousands of virus genomes  and this information has been really  valuable for studying pathogen evolution  and an academic spread so the the next  strain database is the main global  repository for all of these sequences  and tens of thousands of sequences are  now available so  ngs has of course been the the primary  technology used for whole genome  sequencing  and there are many different uh  published protocols on different ngs  platforms that are  available including the iron seek  sauro covt research panel which provides  any of the viral genome  and when it runs on the genexis  integrated sequencer  has a turnaround time of about one day  to produce a full viral sequence  so for many of the ngs protocols sanger  sequencing  plays a role to help gap fill ngs  sequences  and to confirm identified variants in  fact some of the very  early sequencing data from groups in  china used sanghar sequencing to gap  fill the  original ngs data but in addition to  that there are two published protocols  for  complete genome sequencing of the sarco  v2 virus  one from the cdc in in the us and  another from the  cdc in china  so the first genome sanger sequencing  protocol  was published by cdc back in march  shortly after  the first sequence of the virus was  published  the workflow is shown in the top of this  slide it begins with rna extraction  and then an arc  in the case of samples that have  so typically our ct values of 27 and  above would require this second pcr step  um pcr products are purified  via an exosapi purification and then a  cycle sequencing  cycle sequence using that big day  terminator version  3.1 sequencing protocol and then  separated  on the 3730 capillary electrophoresis  instrument  and for the cdc protocol there are 38  primer pairs that are needed to span  the nearly 30 000 bases of the virus and  these are tiled across the virus as  shown in the top of the slide  and in the cases of a low viral load  where a second round nested pcr  is performed primers that sit just  inside the original primers are used  and this produces amplicons in the range  of about 320 to 1 000 base pairs  and then four individual sequencing  reactions are used to sequence both the  forward and the reverse  regions of each pcr product so a total  of 148 sequencing primers are used  and a single sample takes about 18 hours  to sequence via this approach  and as i mentioned the this this  protocol is very useful for detecting  low viral load samples so the second  published protocol  shown in the bottom of the slide is very  similar approach um it tiles  um amplicons across the genome um in  this  case also 38 prime of paris are used uh  producing amplicons in the range of 600  to 1400 base pairs in length  a difference being here is that a rapid  amplification of cd  cdna ends race approaches used to  sequence the  um the three prime and the five prime  end of the virus  um so there are a number of sangha  sequence full genomes in the  in the next strain database um  particularly from regions where access  to expensive ngs instrumentation and  expertise is limited  and also for samples that  are very low viral loads anger  sequencing is often a preferred choice  so beyond sangra beyond sequencing the  entire genome  um sanger also has a significant  role to help understand the host  response to the virus  um the life cycle of sarcob2 infection  begins  with the entry of the virus into the  cell through the h2 receptor that's  shown in the top  uh left of the slide and the sweat  region of the virus binds to ace2  through the receptor binding domain of  the virus  variation in the h2 receptor sequence  can  affect how tightly the virus binds um  it might alter a host susceptibility to  sire cov2 infection  and be responsible for person-to-person  differences that are seen in  infection so studies have shown that  there are about  120 amino acids on the outside of the  outer surface of h2  that span the interface of the receptor  binding domain of a virus  and there are two key hotspot mutations  um here  in the alpha helical bundle and these  are important for  cyrus cov2 binding and these are lysine  31 and lysine 353  and sanger sequencing can be used to  identify different variants  in this region so antibodies  from vaccination or as a result of  infection  um target the receptive binding domain  of the spike protein they neutralize the  virus  by binding in this region and block  entry to the cell  so therapeutic neutralizing antibodies  you know also target this region spiked  protein  and there's somewhere on the order of  about 20 different monoclonal antibodies  um that are that are currently in  clinical trials  um as treatment options for the  infection with coronavirus  and there are many more that are in  pre-clinical development as well  so although mutation rates of the virus  are slow  um some changes in the receptor binding  domain have been seen in some isolates  and it remains important to monitor uh  the virus for changes of sequence in the  receptor binding domain  surveillane is about 183 amino acids and  so it can easily be sequenced by sanger  sequencing  and there's no need to sequence the  entire virus for this purpose  so after the virus has entered the cell  a newly formed virus is ingested by  antigen presenting cells that's shown in  the diagram which go on  to display fragments of the virus and  can activate  t helper cells which then go on to turn  on b  cells which produce antibodies  against the virus or to activate  cytotoxic t cells which can destroy the  virus  so b cells can be isolated from patients  infected with the virus by using the  spike protein as bait  and then a technique like flow cytometry  to isolate  individual b cells and there are  well-established protocols for saying a  sequencing of heavy chain  and light chain regions of antibodies  that have been recovered from b cells  so this is really useful for identifying  type binding antibody sequences  um  finally as we begin to think ahead to  the prospect  of an effective coronavirus vaccine next  year  the question of durability of response  in an individual  is an important question and there's a  growing  body of research that links immune  response to  variations in the microbiome composition  present in our bodies so  there are many there are as many as 100  times more bacterial genes residing in  humans than  human genes and recent data indicates  that gut microbiota can modulate the  response to vaccines  and as we'll see in the next few slides  a fragment analysis bce  approach can provide a rapid  cost-effective approach to  profiling microbiota in the gut and the  airways  so a technique uh known as iso  is pro has been developed by a small  startup company in the netherlands  called in biome  um and their approach is based on the  specific amplification of interspace  regions in bacteria  to generate fluorescently labeled  fragments that can then be separated  on applied biosystems genetic analyzer  instruments so all bacteria  have at least one in the space region  and men and many bacteria have multiple  regions  and is pro relies on length and sequence  polymorphisms  um of these interspace regions to  provide  identification of the bacteria so for  example in the bottom of this slide  show a these interspace regions for  uh this particular bacterial species  enterococcus basalis  it has four different interspace regions  is one is two ice three and is four  uh when uh after amplification of is1  and is2  you get a 377 base spread  base pair fragment um and amplification  of is3 and is4 produces  275 base pair fragment and so each  bacteria has  an almost unique fingerprint when these  interspace regions  are amplified and you can also  group different phylum of bacteria  in by using different labeled  primers so uh three different colors  shown here for three different file  and some of the advantages of this  approach um  are that it's a rapid workflow you can  get  an answer within about a four to five  hour turnaround time  does not require culture of the bacteria  and it can detect unknown and unknown  species  no need to predefine the species that  you're looking for  and in addition um it  can detect bacterial bacteria that  cannot be cultivated so our colleagues  in biome have used this approach to  examine  microbiota and nasopharyngeal swabs  collected from patients the  individuals that have been infected with  osiris-cov2  and they have used this technique to  identify  specific microbiota profiles that may be  correlated to the likelihood of  infection of the virus  they examined 135 different clinical  research samples from people ranging in  age from the very young to the very  elderly in this zero to 100 age  range um and they  um use the ios pro assay to profile um  dna isolated from the nasopharyngeal  frogs  so what you can see on the slide is a  resulting claustrogram  where the pcr status of each individual  is listed either as red or green red  being positive for the virus green being  negative for the virus  and these were independently determined  by qpcr  um and then you can you can see that um  after unsupervised clustering was  performed of all of the profiles there's  a clear  profile of the 77 samples with similar  microbiota profile in the center of the  clustergram here um and  in addition well um  so this cluster of um  this central cluster showed a very  low level or or a relatively low level  of cyrus co2 positivity compared to the  cluster  that was outside of the um  of the central cluster so in the for the  cluster about 25 percent of the  people were cyrus cov2 a positive and  outside of this cluster this rose to 47  percent  and in addition um  data that's not shown in the slide  indicates that older individuals were  actually enriched for this  h signature which poses the question  whether there is an age-dependent  pharyngeal microbiota signature  that has an increased susceptibility so  further and more detailed analysis that  this dataset can be  found in this publication that's listed  on the slide  or alternatively there is a poster that  will also be presented in this  conference that  goes into more detail into the data  analysis and the conclusions  so i want to switch gears now and talk  um a little bit about the use of sanger  said zoonotic um so it's wide  recognition the united disease is the  growing threat in fact the cdc estimates  that more than  every 10 people can be spread from  animals and  in fact three out of four new or  emerging infectious diseases actually  animals into people and they can we can  pass  um either directly um from an animal  into a human for example if we discussed  um andromedas uh we're responsible  uh for directly transferring the  mers virus and of course we have a bird  flu and swine flu that can be directly  transferred into humans  or they can start from a viral source  such as the bat and pass through  another animal either a civet as in the  case of the original sars or  it's postulated that the pangolins are  the intermediary  uh hosts um for the cyclov2 virus  so there's a growing need for assays  that can detect pathogens  that can pass from humans to animals to  humans  and also to identify the natural  reservoir for a particular pathogen that  has  um the possibility of infecting human  population  so sequencing can be used to address  this  to provide information on pathogens in  animal and animal hosts that are  closely related to humanized versions  and more likely to cross the  species boundary so one  approach that has been used for this  purpose  that is has capability to  identify all currently recognized and  potentially future  emergent coronaviruses is an rt pcr  amplification based approach  followed by sequencing of a particular  region of the coronavirus this  rna-dependent  rna polymerase region of the virus in  the orth  1ab region and this approach is  described in the publication listed at  the top of the slide here in the journal  of clinical  virology but has been widely used by  others as well  the approach consists of two different  pcrs that use degenerate primers  a first pcr that produces  an ampliton about 670 bases in length  with degenerate forward and reverse  primers and then a second pcr  that is a nested approach that uh  generates a smaller fragment  again using a degenerate primer um of  about 600 base pairs and this  amplicon can then be uh sanger sequenced  and to confirm the identity so with this  approach you know there are a large  number of coronaviruses that can be  identified  um and the assay shows uh  virtually no cross-reactivity with other  common respiratory pathogens such as rsv  or flu  and it can be used to routinely screen  uh individuals um whose occupation bring  them into close contact with animals  for prolonged periods of time or it can  be used for  routine surveillance in species such as  bats  which are reservoirs for viruses with  human pathogenic potential  so finally i'd like to briefly describe  an application of ce and the study of  sara cov  to detection that is based on a fragment  analysis approach  the workflow for ce fragment analysis  detection is actually similar to qpcr  it starts with a collection of the  sample in a  via nasopharyngeal swab and then  extraction  of the rna for example using  one of the kits from thermo fissure a  magmax viral pathogen nucleic acid  isolation kit  and then after rna extraction and rt pcr  step is performed with fluorescently  labeled primers um and at the end of  this the  uh fragments fleshly labeled fragments  that  have been amplified are identified  on the ce and the size of the fragment  and the color of the fragment  encodes data about which amplicon is  being detected  and the data can be analyzed via a  software such as a  gmat thermofisher so for the assay  developed at thermofisher there were  three different regions of the virus  that were amplified  one from the off-1ab region the second  from the spike gene and the third from  the nuclear capsid gene  and this results in three different size  fragments  an n protein fragment at 396 base pairs  and off 1ab  425 and s protein at 437 base pairs  and additionally an rna positive control  is added  to the reaction and to help check for  the presence of  any pcr inhibitors that might prevent  amplification  so one added feature of this assay that  i'd like to point out  is the use of multiple fluorescently  labeled primers  this allows after amplification for the  samples to be combined to be pooled  together  such that four individual reactions can  be run  on the capillary combined and read out  and run on the ce  and the fragments are then identified  based on not only the size but also the  color so  each capillary can then produce answers  for for the application from four  individuals  and the sensitivity of this approach is  is similar to qpcr methods  and more details on the protocol  and an application note can be found on  the thermofisher website as shown on the  slide  um including the sequences of all of the  primers  that are used in the assay  so by way of summary um  what i've hopefully what i've shown you  is that sanger sequencing and fragment  analysis technologies  are widely useful in the detection and  sequencing of  cov2 sangro sequencing can be used to  support  an ngs whole genome sequencing  by gap filling and confirmation of  variants but can also be used  to generate a whole genome sequence  um by itself um targeted sequencing you  know can be 