 cluster about 25 percent of the  people were cyrus cov2 a positive and  outside of this cluster this rose to 47  percent  and in addition um  data that's not shown in the slide  indicates that older individuals were  actually enriched for this  h signature which poses the question  whether there is an age-dependent  pharyngeal microbiota signature  that has an increased susceptibility so  further and more detailed analysis that  this dataset can be  found in this publication that's listed  on the slide  or alternatively there is a poster that  will also be presented in this  conference that  goes into more detail into the data  analysis and the conclusions  so i want to switch gears now and talk  um a little bit about the use of sanger  said zoonotic um so it's wide  recognition the united disease is the  growing threat in fact the cdc estimates  that more than  every 10 people can be spread from  animals and  in fact three out of four new or  emerging infectious diseases actually  animals into people and they can we can  pass  um either directly um from an animal  into a human for example if we discussed  um andromedas uh we're responsible  uh for directly transferring the  mers virus and of course we have a bird  flu and swine flu that can be directly  transferred into humans  or they can start from a viral source  such as the bat and pass through  another animal either a civet as in the  case of the original sars or  it's postulated that the pangolins are  the intermediary  uh hosts um for the cyclov2 virus  so there's a growing need for assays  that can detect pathogens  that can pass from humans to animals to  humans  and also to identify the natural  reservoir for a particular pathogen that  has  um the possibility of infecting human  population  so sequencing can be used to address  this  to provide information on pathogens in  animal and animal hosts that are  closely related to humanized versions  and more likely to cross the  species boundary so one  approach that has been used for this  purpose  that is has capability to  identify all currently recognized and  potentially future  emergent coronaviruses is an rt pcr  amplification based approach  followed by sequencing of a particular  region of the coronavirus this  rna-dependent  rna polymerase region of the virus in  the orth  1ab region and this approach is  described in the publication listed at  the top of the slide here in the journal  of clinical  virology but has been widely used by  others as well  the approach consists of two different  pcrs that use degenerate primers  a first pcr that produces  an ampliton about 670 bases in length  with degenerate forward and reverse  primers and then a second pcr  that is a nested approach that uh  generates a smaller fragment  again using a degenerate primer um of  about 600 base pairs and this  amplicon can then be uh sanger sequenced  and to confirm the identity so with this  approach you know there are a large  number of coronaviruses that can be  identified  um and the assay shows uh  virtually no cross-reactivity with other  common respiratory pathogens such as rsv  or flu  and it can be used to routinely screen  uh individuals um whose occupation bring  them into close contact with animals  for prolonged periods of time or it can  be used for  routine surveillance in species such as  bats  which are reservoirs for viruses with  human pathogenic potential  so finally i'd like to briefly describe  an application of ce and the study of  sara cov  to detection that is based on a fragment  analysis approach  the workflow for ce fragment analysis  detection is actually similar to qpcr  it starts with a collection of the  sample in a  via nasopharyngeal swab and then  extraction  of the rna for example using  one of the kits from thermo fissure a  magmax viral pathogen nucleic acid  isolation kit  and then after rna extraction and rt pcr  step is performed with fluorescently  labeled primers um and at the end of  this the  uh fragments fleshly labeled fragments  that  have been amplified are identified  on the ce and the size of the fragment  and the color of the fragment  encodes data about which amplicon is  being detected  and the data can be analyzed via a  software such as a  gmat thermofisher so for the assay  developed at thermofisher there were  three different regions of the virus  that were amplified  one from the off-1ab region the second  from the spike gene and the third from  the nuclear capsid gene  and this results in three different size  fragments  an n protein fragment at 396 base pairs  and off 1ab  425 and s protein at 437 base pairs  and additionally an rna positive control  is added  to the reaction and to help check for  the presence of  any pcr inhibitors that might prevent  amplification  so one added feature of this assay that  i'd like to point out  is the use of multiple fluorescently  labeled primers  this allows after amplification for the  samples to be combined to be pooled  together  such that four individual reactions can  be run  on the capillary combined and read out  and run on the ce  and the fragments are then identified  based on not only the size but also the  color so  each capillary can then produce answers  for for the application from four  individuals  and the sensitivity of this approach is  is similar to qpcr methods  and more details on the protocol  and an application note can be found on  the thermofisher website as shown on the  slide  um including the sequences of all of the  primers  that are used in the assay  so by way of summary um  what i've hopefully what i've shown you  is that sanger sequencing and fragment  analysis technologies  are widely useful in the detection and  sequencing of  cov2 sangro sequencing can be used to  support  an ngs whole genome sequencing  by gap filling and confirmation of  variants but can also be used  to generate a whole genome sequence  um by itself um targeted sequencing you  know can be used  to look at v2 host response factors such  as  a sequence of h2 receptors  which the virus binds to to gain entry  to the cell or can be used to  look at the immune response  look at the antibody sequences that are  produced from individual  b cells by sequencing of the variable  regions  um there has been an assay developed  that amplifies and sequences a highly  conserved region  um of the virus the rdpr  rprp region and this can be useful  to identify the natural reservoir of the  virus or to help track  animal coronaviruses that may jump to  species  and then finally bacterial profiling  using the is pro technology  can develop a more comprehensive  understanding of how the microbiome can  influence host response to pathogen  infection  so with that i i'll conclude my talk and  invite any questions and thank you for  your time
 

